course: biology
lesson: 379
title: China's great rivers: the finless porpoise, the lost baiji, Chinese alligators and sturgeon
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: The Yangtze is the longest river in Asia, about 6,300 kilometres, with hundreds of millions of people along it and huge ships going up and down. For the animals living in the water it is busy, noisy and crowded.
explain: A grey porpoise with a smiling face and no back fin still lives there. Its cousin the baiji, a pale river dolphin with a long thin beak, was searched for in 2006 and none were found: it is probably the first dolphin people drove to extinction.
explain: The baiji found fish by sending clicks and listening for echoes, and ship engines may have drowned them out. Nets, electric fishing and dams did the rest, and the giant Chinese paddlefish was lost too.
explain: Now China has banned fishing on the main Yangtze for ten years, and the small Chinese alligator, which may have helped inspire dragon legends, is being bred and released. Saving a river means looking after the whole river, not one animal.
fact: The Yangtze is about 6,300 kilometres long, the longest river in Asia and the third longest in the world, and hundreds of millions of people live along it.
fact: The Yangtze finless porpoise has a low ridge with tiny bumps instead of a back fin and is the only porpoise that lives in fresh water.
fact: A 2022 survey counted about 1,250 finless porpoises, slightly more than five years before.
fact: Every few years teams sail the whole Yangtze and count porpoises by sight and with underwater microphones that hear their clicks, counting the same way each time to show if numbers go up or down.
fact: The baiji, the Yangtze river dolphin, was called the goddess of the Yangtze in legends; a big search in 2006 found none, and scientists said it was probably extinct.
fact: It may be the first dolphin driven extinct by people, through boats, nets, electric fishing and dams.
fact: The baiji had tiny eyes and was almost blind in the muddy river; it found fish by sending clicks and listening for echoes, and ship engine noise may have drowned these out.
fact: In 2021 China banned fishing on the main Yangtze and many lakes for ten years, to let fish recover, which also feeds porpoises; scientists report more fish and porpoises in some places since.
fact: There are only two kinds of alligator in the world, the American and the Chinese.
fact: The Chinese alligator is small, about 1.5 metres, spends the winter asleep in burrows, and only a few hundred live wild, but zoos have bred many and some were released.
fact: Some people think the Chinese alligator helped inspire dragon legends, but nobody knows for sure; Chinese dragons bring rain and live in rivers, and old books describe a river dragon called tuo.
fact: The Chinese paddlefish could grow several metres long and was declared extinct in 2020, after dams blocked it from the places where it laid eggs.
fact: The Three Gorges Dam on the Yangtze is the biggest power station in the world by power.
fact: Chinese sturgeon live in the sea and swim up the Yangtze to lay eggs, but dams now block their old places; in some recent years no wild breeding was seen, and people breed and release them.
exercise: How long is the Yangtze? | About 6,300 kilometres | Only about 63 kilometres | Only about 630 kilometres | About 63,000 kilometres
exercise: What has the Yangtze finless porpoise instead of a back fin? | A low ridge with tiny bumps | Two small back fins side by side on its back | A tall pointed fin like a shark's | A hump like a camel's, on its back
exercise: What is special about the Yangtze finless porpoise? | It is the only porpoise that lives in fresh water | It is the biggest whale in the world, longer than a bus or two | It can walk on land | It lives only in the sea
exercise: About how many finless porpoises did the 2022 survey count? | About 1,250 | Only about 12 | About 125,000 | None at all, they died out
exercise: How do survey teams count porpoises they cannot see? | With underwater microphones that hear their clicks | By counting their footprints | By catching them all in nets and putting a ring on each one | By asking fishermen to guess
exercise: Why must the counting method stay the same every time? | So the numbers can be compared to see if they go up or down | Because the boat cannot be changed | Because porpoises learn new methods | So the count is always the same number
exercise: What happened when scientists searched for the baiji in 2006? | They found none, and it was probably extinct | They found thousands swimming in the river near Wuhan | They found it living in the sea | They found it in a zoo
exercise: What drove the baiji to extinction? | Boats, nets, electric fishing and dams | Cold winters only | Sharks swimming up the river from the sea to hunt them | Old age of the last ones
exercise: How did the almost blind baiji find fish? | By sending clicks and listening for echoes | By smelling the water | By feeling for them with its long flippers and fins | By following birds
exercise: When did China ban fishing on the main Yangtze for ten years? | In 2021 | In 1921 | In 2001 | In 2061
exercise: How many kinds of alligator are there in the world? | Two, the American and the Chinese | One, only the American kind in Florida | Ten kinds | Hundreds of kinds
exercise: How long is a Chinese alligator? | About 1.5 metres | About 15 metres long | About 15 centimetres | About 6 metres, longer than a car
exercise: Where does a Chinese alligator spend the winter? | Asleep in a burrow | Swimming in the sea | Sunbathing in Africa | Awake under the ice
exercise: Was the Chinese dragon based on the Chinese alligator? | Some think it helped inspire dragons, but nobody knows for sure | Yes, it is proved | No, dragons were real animals that lived along the Yangtze and died out | Yes, alligators can fly
exercise: What was the Chinese paddlefish? | A fish several metres long with a sword-like nose, now extinct | A small pet fish that people keep in garden ponds and fish tanks | A bird that paddles on rivers | A boat used for fishing
exercise: What is the Three Gorges Dam? | The biggest power station in the world by power | The biggest fish farm in China, full of sturgeon | An old stone wall built by an emperor to keep out enemies | A long bridge carrying fast trains over the Yangtze
exercise: Why are Chinese sturgeon struggling? | Dams block the places where they used to lay eggs | They have no fins | They are eaten by porpoises | The sea is too fresh for them, so they cannot grow big or strong
test: Count them: a survey boat sees 3 finless porpoises and 2 dolphins from the sea. How many back fins? | 2 | 5 | 3 | 0
test: Count them: there are 2 kinds of alligator. How many kinds live in China? | 1 | 2 | 0 | 3
test: Count them: a fishing ban starts in 2021 and lasts 10 years. In which year does it end? | 2031 | 2030 | 2011 | 2041
test: In a cartoon a river dolphin looks for fish with big shining eyes. What is right about the baiji? | It was almost blind and found fish by clicks and echoes | It had huge eyes that glowed in the dark muddy water | It hunted only in clear water | It found fish by smell alone
test: «the porpoise isn't even caught, so why would a fishing ban help it» What is right? | More fish in the river means more food, and fewer nets to tangle in | Porpoises eat fishing nets | The ban makes porpoises grow fins | It would not help at all, because nets and fishing boats never hurt porpoises
test: How would you find your way if loud engine noise filled your ears and you used sound to see? | You would get lost, like a baiji whose echoes were drowned out | You would see better | Noise makes echoes louder | You would just use your eyes instead, as baiji did in muddy water
test: «dams are clean power so they're all good, end of» What is the careful answer? | Dams make clean power but can block fish from where they lay eggs | Dams never harm anything, because the water still flows through them | Dams make rivers dirtier only | Dams help fish lay more eggs
test: What could have been done 30 years earlier to save the baiji? | Limit nets, electric fishing and boat noise and protect its river | Teach it to see in the dark | Move all the last baiji far away to the salty Black Sea, where boats are fewer | Paint the river blue
test: «chinese alligator, so it's huge like the american ones» What is right? | It is small, about 1.5 metres | It is the biggest reptile in the world | It is as long and heavy as a bus | It is a kind of lizard that flies
test: Why might a dam harm a fish that lives far downstream? | It blocks the fish from swimming up to where it lays eggs | Dams make the fish forget the way | Dams drink all the water | Fish are scared of the dam's dark shadow and will not swim anywhere near it
test: Why might noisy ships harm a porpoise even if they never hit it? | It clicks to find fish, and engine noise can drown out the echoes | Noise makes the river water too warm | Porpoises hate the colour of ships | Ships drink up the river
exam: Identify the principal method by which the baiji located its prey within the turbid waters of the Yangtze. | By the emission of clicks and the interpretation of returning echoes | By acute vision | By the sense of smell alone | By tracking the movement of birds
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the Yangtze finless porpoise. | It is the sole porpoise of fresh water and bears a low ridge instead of a dorsal fin | It inhabits the open ocean exclusively | It possesses a tall dorsal fin | It has been extinct since 2006
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the number of extant species of alligator? | Two species are extant, the American and the Chinese | A single species is extant, namely the American | Ten species are extant | No species of alligator survives in the wild
exam: Ascertain the principal cause of the decline of the Chinese sturgeon, as presented in this lesson. | Dams obstruct its access to its former spawning grounds | Predation by finless porpoises | Excessive freshness of sea water at the mouth of the Yangtze | A deficiency of salt in its diet
